-- title:
--   Inertia at q acting non-trivially on a primitive eigenquotient
-- statement:
--   Let $M\ge 1$, let $\varepsilon$ be a Dirichlet character mod $M$ with values in $\mathbb{C}$ and let $g$ be a cusp form of weight $2$ for $\Gamma_1(M)$ which is primitive for $\varepsilon$: its first $q$-coefficient is $1$, it satisfies the Hecke multiplicativity relations $a_{\ell n}+\varepsilon(\ell)\ell^{k-1}a_{n/\ell}=a_\ell a_n$ at primes $\ell\nmid M$ and $a_{\ell n}=a_\ell a_n$ at primes $\ell\mid M$, it has nebentypus $\varepsilon$, and its eigenpacket occurs at no proper divisor level $M'\mid M$. Let $p$ be a prime, assume the inputs [`ModularCurve.HeckeDiamondInputsAll M`](def/ModularCurve_X1HeckeModule.html#L58) providing the Hecke correspondences at each prime and the diamond automorphisms for each $d$ coprime to $M$, and assume [`ModularCurve.HeckeDiamondCommuteBar M`](def/ModularCurve_X1HeckeModule.html#L54), that the Hecke and diamond endomorphisms of $J_1(M)=\mathrm{Pic}^0$ of the base-changed function field of $X_1(M)$ pairwise commute, so that $J_1(M)$ is a module over [`ModularCurve.HeckeAlgOne`](def/ModularCurve_X1HeckeModule.html#L16), the polynomial ring over $\mathbb{Z}$ on symbols $T_\ell$ ($\ell$ prime) and $\langle d\rangle$ ($d\in\mathbb{N}$). Let $S$ be a finite set of naturals, $K$ a field of characteristic zero that is a $\mathbb{Z}_p$-algebra, $R$ a commutative ring with an injective ring homomorphism $\mathrm{toC}:R\to\mathbb{C}$ and a ring homomorphism $\psi:R\to K$, and $b,e:\mathbb{N}\to R$ with $\mathrm{toC}(b(\ell))=a_\ell(g)$ and $\mathrm{toC}(e(\ell))=\varepsilon(\ell)$ for all primes $\ell\nmid M$ outside $S$. Let $q$ be a prime with $q\neq p$, $q\mid M$, $q^2\nmid M$ and $q\nmid\mathrm{cond}(\varepsilon)$. Then for every ring homomorphism $\Lambda$ from the $\mathbb{Q}_p$-subalgebra of $\mathrm{End}_{\mathbb{Q}_p}(\mathbb{Q}_p\otimes_{\mathbb{Z}_p}T_p J_1(M))$ generated by the image of the Hecke algebra to $K$ which agrees with the structure map on $\mathbb{Z}_p\subset\mathbb{Q}_p$ and satisfies $\Lambda(T_\ell)\psi(e(\ell))=\psi(b(\ell))$ and $\Lambda(\langle\ell\rangle)\psi(e(\ell))=1$ for all primes $\ell\nmid M$ outside $S$, and for every valuation subring $P$ of an algebraic closure of $\mathbb{Q}$ with $q$ a non-unit of $P$, there are an element $\sigma$ of the image in $\mathrm{Gal}(\overline{\mathbb{Q}}/\mathbb{Q})$ of the inertia subgroup of $P$ and an element $x$ of the Tate module $T_p J_1(M)$ (sequences $(x_n)$ in $J_1(M)$ with $p^n x_n=0$ and $p\,x_{n+1}=x_n$) such that $1\otimes(\sigma x-x)$ in $K\otimes_{\mathbb{Z}_p}T_pJ_1(M)$ does not lie in the $K$-span of the elements $t\cdot y-\Lambda(t)\,y$ for $t$ in the Hecke algebra and $y$ in $K\otimes_{\mathbb{Z}_p}T_pJ_1(M)$.
--
--   This is the ramification assertion at a prime exactly dividing the level in the local description of the Galois representation attached to a newform, as in Darmon–Diamond–Taylor, Theorem 3.1(e), and in Carayol's theorem identifying the conductor with the level: the inertia group at $q$ does not act trivially on the quotient of $K\otimes T_pJ_1(M)$ on which the Hecke algebra acts through $\Lambda$. It is used in the computation of characteristic polynomials of Frobenius elements for the $p$-adic representation attached to a primitive form of level divisible exactly once by $q$ with nebentypus unramified at $q$.
